## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical React Native fetch patterns: cancellation prevents stale data and memory leaks during auto-refresh and manual refresh. It also checks hook design and safe state updates in unmount scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- AbortController usage in fetch calls
- useEffect cleanup and interval management
- Race condition avoidance with latest-request checks
- Small custom hook design for reusable cancellation logic

## Code Example

```javascript
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';

export function useCancelableFetch(url) {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const currentReq = useRef(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isActive = true;
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    const id = ++currentReq.current;
    setLoading(true);
    fetch(url, { signal: ctrl.signal })
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(d => {
        if (isActive && id === currentReq.current) {
          setData(d);
          setLoading(false);
        }
      })
      .catch(e => {
        if (isActive && e.name !== 'AbortError') setError(e);
        if (isActive) setLoading(false);
      });
    return () => {
      isActive = false;
      ctrl.abort();
    };
  }, [url]);

  return { data, loading, error };
}
```

